---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Study from "../components/Study.astro";

// TO-WRITE:
// Nothing for now
---

<Layout title="Studies" locale="en">
    <section class="mt-20">
        <h1>Studies</h1>
        <p>
            This is a record of my studies. I can't document every single
            project or course I have done, but I will keep the most important.
            The dates are completion dates, but most of them started months or
            years earlier.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-8">
        <Study title="Liminal Raycast" type="Project" date="2026-10-25">
            This was my first real project in C, i used SDL3 and raycasting to
            make a simple maze game. However i tried to innovate a little adding
            non-euclidean spaces. It was really nice to work on such low-level
            libraries, crafting graphics and geometry bit by bit.
        </Study>

        <Study
            title="Technology as a service playbook"
            type="Book"
            date="2026-10-14"
        >
            This book was full of incredible useful information. It explores the
            fundamentals of managing and scaling a XaaS business. I would highly
            recommend it to anyone in a managing position.
        </Study>

        <Study title="Capucare" type="Project" date="2026-10-08">
            My second shipped platform, a non-profit for animal shelters. It's
            very similar to Capupet on the technical and design side. However,
            it brought a lot of challenges in the business logic.
        </Study>

        <Study title="Capupet" type="Project" date="2026-10-03">
            This is the first real SaaS I shipped. I learned a lot about code
            quality, performance and how to write reliable software in general.
            I also learned a lot about the business side and UI/UX design.
        </Study>

        <Study title="Factory ERP" type="Project" date="2026-07-22">
            This was my main project at my first full-time job. I spent a couple
            of years working on an ERP that could manage all the factory
            processes. I learned a lot about how a factory works. I also learned
            a TON about advanced SQL and data modeling.
        </Study>

        <Study title="Churchpaq" type="Project" date="2026-06-01">
            This was my first try at building a real SaaS. I learned a lot about
            fullstack development, design, mobile development, scaling,
            performance, and much more. However, I ended up ditching it.
        </Study>

        <Study title="System Design Interview" type="Book" date="2026-04-20">
            It's an amazing book by ByteByteGo. It showed me the basics of
            distributed systems and how to design for scale.
        </Study>

        <Study title="Geometry Bullets" type="Project" date="2026-03-24">
            I decided to remake my 2D shooter, this time in JS. I used 0
            dependencies. The whole game is running in an HTML canvas. Physics,
            collisions, movement, and rendering were all coded from scratch.
        </Study>

        <Study title="The Pragmatic Programmer" type="Book" date="2026-03-19">
            This was my first coding book. Since I'm more of a practical
            learner, it's more about how to be a good craftsman than about
            coding concepts. I'd rate it 10/10 and recommend it to any
            programmer.
        </Study>

        <Study title="IT Management" type="Project" date="2025-02-01">
            My first full-time job was at 16, in a medium-sized factory in
            Tijuana. I was the IT admin of the company. I learned a lot in this
            job, and had a real challenge. Previous IT managers didn't do a nice
            job, so it took me around a year to fix and standardize everything.
        </Study>

        <Study title="Custom websites" type="Project" date="2023-09-22">
            My first real job as a programmer was as a freelancer making custom
            websites for coffee shops and photographers. I did a lot of practice
            and designs. However, I only got 1 client.
        </Study>

        <Study
            title="National math contest (Cecyte)"
            type="Contest"
            date="2022-05-30"
        >
            In my first year of high school, a teacher said a math contest was
            worth an extra point. I entered, won the state round, and flew to
            Durango for nationals, where I placed third. I barely studied. That
            made me realize my own potential and made me wonder what I could
            achieve if I really studied. I later left high school so I could
            spend the time studying programming by myself.
        </Study>

        <Study
            title="Geometry and bullets (Unity)"
            type="Project"
            date="2022-02-14"
        >
            I decided I was ready for real code. I was learning geometry and
            trigonometry. I opened Unity and made a full 2D shooter, which was
            actually pretty fun. However, instead of using the physics engine, I
            developed every movement andtrajectory using my own math.
        </Study>

        <Study title="Raycast in Scratch" type="Project" date="2020-06-01">
            I wanted a challenge in Scratch. I was curious about making 3D
            games. I tried some techniques, but my favourite was Doom's raycast.
            I learned the fundamentals and created a 3D maze. This would be my
            "final test" in Scratch.
        </Study>

        <Study title="Scratch projects" type="Project" date="2019-02-14">
            My first approach to programming was in Scratch. My teacher in 6th
            grade showed us the tool and how to move our character around the
            screen. However, I was left amazed and started playing and trying to
            make games myself. After some years I developed Cookie Clicker and
            Pou clones.
        </Study>
    </section>
</Layout>
